    "rationale": "Iteration 0 (48 mm span, 12 mm footprints, lead 0.05 m, track 0.12 m) scored 79.6. Iteration 1 changed four things at once (72 mm span, 16 mm footprints, lead 0.03 m, track 0.10 m) and scored 56.2. Every trial in both designs ended off_track with max_error about 0.061 m, which is the 0.06 m off-track floor. The line-loss counts (1 to 2 per run) were not cleared by the wider span, and the s-bend progress fell from 0.29 to 0.18 after the lead was shortened. I therefore read the lead reduction and the narrower track as harmful, and the span as not yet tested properly.

Geometry check. A sensor at lateral position x sees the tape only if the tape overlaps its footprint. With tape half-width 0.0125 m and a robot offset d, the tape spans d-0.0125 to d+0.0125. In iteration 1 the outermost sensor was at x = 0.036 with a 16 mm footprint, so it covers 0.028 to 0.044 and loses sight of the tape once d exceeds about 0.0565 m. That matches the observed failure point, so the line was lost just before the 0.06 m limit. Widening the span to reach about 0.048 m keeps the outer sensor on the tape until d is about 0.0685 m, which is past the off-track limit. Body width does not help: the off-track limit is max(0.06, 0.0125 + body_width/4), and even the 0.18 m maximum body gives 0.0575 m, which is still below the 0.06 m floor. The Integrator's note that widening the body raises the limit to 0.0575 m is correct but does not change the outcome.
